                    "A survey is evidence of where boundaries and other physical features are located, while a deed and other recorded instruments establish or limit legal interests. The surveyor’s work product should be read with its stated basis, date, and limitations. A boundary corner may be marked by a monument, iron pin, monumented distance, or occupation; the most convincing result often combines physical evidence with documentary research. Fences, tree lines, driveways, and old corners can help retrace a boundary but can also reflect convenience or a later mistake. Do not move a fence, cut a tree, or stake a project line before the relevant review and written instructions are complete.",
                    "Encumbrances include claims that reduce the value, flexibility, or transferability of land. A mortgage or deed of trust is a financial lien, a mechanic’s lien may reflect work performed on the property, and a tax lien may be recorded by a taxing authority. A restrictive covenant can limit building height, setbacks, uses, or subdivision, while a mineral interest may create rights to explore, develop, or remove subsurface resources. Leases can affect possession even when fee title appears clear. For each item, record the claimant, legal basis, current status, effect, and document that would release or clarify it. A title report may list an item without resolving whether it is presently enforceable.",
