                    "Owner financing replaces some or all of the cash required to purchase land with a loan from the seller or another lender. The basic documents usually include a promissory note, a security agreement, and a recorded mortgage or deed of trust, but the correct package depends on the jurisdiction and transaction. The note states the promise to pay; the security document creates or confirms a collateral interest in the property. Review principal, interest, payment frequency, amortization, maturity, prepayment, default, collateral, insurance, and due-on-sale terms separately. A promissory note is not a complete land contract, and a down payment is not a defense against a later title or lien problem. Have counsel review the documents as a package and confirm the state’s lending, usury, foreclosure, and licensing rules.",
                    "Interest can be simple, compound, fixed, variable, paid in cash, or deferred. A note that says interest is paid monthly but has no amortization may produce a balloon, while an amortizing note may have a large final payment after regular installments. Calculate a full payment schedule, not just the monthly rate, and identify the day-count convention, payment priority, and treatment of extra payments. Confirm whether the lender can release the security interest, substitute collateral, or require additional insurance. If the borrower sells the land, a due-on-sale clause may accelerate the note, but enforceability and protections can depend on the document and applicable law. These are contract and local-law questions, not universal defaults.",
                    "Owner financing changes the risk profile of acquisition. The buyer may reduce upfront cash but accept concentration risk, title risk, servicing effort, and potentially unfavorable terms negotiated under time pressure. The seller may prefer a loan but be inexperienced in foreclosure, accounting, or lien releases. Define who holds the note, where payments are sent, how escrow is handled, what happens after a default, and how a cure or workout is documented. Consider title insurance, an appraisal, insurance, environmental protection, and a policy for receiving payments. A loan secured by land may still be subject to prior liens, and a seller’s promise to pay does not replace clear title or careful borrower review. Financing terms should be evaluated alongside the land’s exit plan.",
