                    "A like-kind exchange, commonly called a 1031 exchange in U.S. tax practice, can allow eligible taxpayers to defer recognized gain when real property held for investment is exchanged for other real property held for investment. The rules are technical and can change, so this chapter provides a planning framework rather than tax advice. Confirm the taxpayer, property, use, ownership, holding period, replacement deadline, intermediary, and any boot. The replacement property must satisfy the applicable timing and use requirements, and the parties must receive value in a qualified intermediary arrangement when required. Do not assume that a sale followed by a later purchase automatically qualifies. A tax professional should review the facts before the seller relies on deferred treatment.",
                    "The exchange process has deadlines and dependencies that must appear in the transaction calendar. The property sold and replacement property must be identified according to the applicable rules, and the qualified intermediary may need to receive the proceeds and hold them under a written agreement. A replacement property can create a new closing, inspection, title, financing, and environmental workstream. A buyer who plans to use the replacement for a different purpose may create an issue with qualified use. Ownership, related-party, entity, and installment-sale questions can also affect the analysis. Keep the exchange agreement, intermediary instructions, title documents, closing statement, and replacement closing together so the tax and land records tell the same story.",
                    "Boot is value received in cash or other nonqualified property. The presence of boot does not automatically eliminate an exchange, but it can affect the amount and character of recognized gain. Do not calculate a tax result from a land model without the basis, holding period, liabilities, expenses, and current law. The investor can instead model cash flow and timing: sale proceeds, intermediary holding, acquisition price, financing, renovation, and the amount of equity left in the replacement property. Compare an exchange scenario with an ordinary sale and reinvestment. The comparison is a planning judgment, and a tax adviser should quantify any difference using the actual transaction facts.",
